 they're gunning for the strike  it's completely unnecessary there's a  sensible pay deal there's a sensible i  think modernization of the railway which  would enable much more flexibility and  much more automation and much safer  railway but the unions need to  understand the world has changed and  people don't need to necessarily travel  as they did in the past which is um  unfortunate  we want people back at work as the  government's been  saying we think it's a better  environment but people many people will  be able to function from home and the  unions don't seem to realize that people  don't need the products in quite the  same way as they once did now the  government is looking at changing some  legislation around strikes so allowing  companies to cover striking workers with  agency staff also potentially increasing  the maximum damages that businesses can  claim for illegal strikes up to a  million pounds is that correct and how  quickly could it be brought in yeah  that's correct um minimum service levels  as well which was in our manifesto which  would mean that certain levels of  service would need to be maintained even  on a strike day what you described as  agency workers coming in is slightly  incorrect what we're actually talking  about is for example if you had somebody  in an operations room at the moment they  can be looking at one screen they won't  move around to another screen because uh  the union rules forbid it as it happens  unless you make them a payment at which  point they say it's safe and they can do  it  what we're talking about is requiring  that kind of flexibility so it's more  transferable skills uh than it is agency  work um in this case  and a range of other things as i say  much of which really doesn't need to  happen if we could just get to a  sensible settlement here but i'm afraid  they are gunning for this um strike as  we've seen by them walking out of the  talks yesterday to attend a rally  uh unfortunately i think therefore we  need to protect the traveling public  um i want to talk to you a bit about  what's happening at the airports as well  uh anyone who has been on holiday or  travelled recently will have seen  firsthand the chaos that is going on  um gatwick has said it's going to reduce  capacity in the summer which means more  cancelled flights are you concerned that  other  airports could do the same thing and how  long could we see this going on for  well we've certainly seen a difficult  bounce back you'll recall both  at easter but also at the whitson uh  holidays those those scenes uh we've  said to the aviation sector that can't  carry on uh and we have a working group  set up with them which is meeting all  the time working very closely with the  aviation um sector we have a series of  different uh things that we're going to  be putting in place and asking them to  do one of which was to ask them to  review their schedules now not on the  day of the flight or the day before  where they destroy somebody's holiday  but in advance and actually i i welcome  uh what gatwick have done to sensibly  look at the schedule and see whether it  could actually be fulfilled  and give people notice now rather than  in six weeks time five weeks time uh  which is what they've done i think  that's smart we'll be coming forward  with some further um ideas to help  alleviate pressure fundamentally of  course it's down to the industry they  need to make sure they employ people get  them in the right places this reminds me  a lot of the problem that we had with a  shortage of lorry uh drivers which we  fixed through 33 different measures uh  that the government and the sector  brought together uh brought forward  together and i'd like to do the same in  the aviation sector  well i'll just give you to give you one  example something we already did which  was make it easier and faster to employ  people getting through the security  referencing system um so we made a  change in the law there and some  technical changes to make it easier to  do that  and and there are more measures along  those types of lines from a government  point of view  and then from an industry point of view  things like them looking at their  schedule as they've done is very helpful  but we'll be saying more about this  actually in the coming week uh i'll say  more about this um but we're we're  watching it like a hawk working very  closely with the aviation sector they  had a stop start two years i understand  how they've got to this position what we  need to do is fix it for uh holiday  makers and people who want to travel by  air and this summer  it feels um just look at the jobs market  a bit more widely um it feels like we're  in a strange situation where it's almost  as if the jobs market is a bit too hot  and we've got 1.3 million vacancies in  the labour market the issue of course is  getting people to fill the jobs and paul  scully uh the minister told sky news  this week that the answer could be  trying to get people to be a bit more  productive so if they're part-time  seeing if they want to work more hours  he was talking about people who've  recalibrated what they want to do when  on furlough  do you agree with him  what i would say actually is i i think  the solution in aviation as in the rest  of the economy is actually not  dissimilar to the way that we've solved  uh or on our way to solving i should  probably say the hgv lorry crisis which  was causing so many problems you  remember even including with tankers at  the  petrol pumps uh and we did that by  looking at the entire market for those  employees paying a little bit more  actually often that was at the at the  very heart of it making sure people were  attracted to the industry just this week  i've launched something called  generation logistics so i think those  are the types of things which help to  resolve this problem as what you  described is an incredibly tight jobs  market i did listen to your introduction  actually and you're saying oh it's a bit  like the 70s well the big difference is  unemployment was a big problem uh  certainly later in the 70s uh what we've  got now is an economy which is  unfortunately very very high levels of  employment and very low unemployment so  i do think continuously skilling people  up more automation  things like that help with productivity  but also just making sure that each  sector is attractive enough to go into  and if you look at baggage handlers for  example i don't think they've had the  best deals and the best packages in the  past i think we've got to make those  jobs uh worthwhile well paid  and  uncomfortable jobs to do a bit more  attractive uh to encourage people to do  them understood um just finally  during boris johnson's leadership  campaign  you were famously the man with the  spreadsheet you predicted to the number  the number of uh conservative mps who  get it back at boris johnson's  leadership campaign  during the recent confidence vote when  148 mps uh voted against boris johnson  did number 10 call on your services  again  but i did make a prediction but i have  to say actually it's a it's a  statistical prediction so in fact  that doesn't tell you was it was it  correct did you get all the money again  i did um but but but you did get it on  the network  sorry you so you correctly predicted 148  mps again  um  one out of five but uh but but uh i  should point out it's not as useful as  it sounds it's not as good as it sounds  it's a statistical prediction rather  than a individuals who voted  specifically in one way uh or the others  i do not want to over uh play the uh the  advantage of this because most whips  want to know who was involved whereas my  outsider looking in uh version of it was  look this is the picture as as i see it  on the subject itself getting getting  getting just one out you did much better  than number 10 who was seemingly  predicting you know far less uh mps were  going to rebel than they did did you  tell boris johnson about your prediction  did you warn him  well well look i'm not going to get into  sort of details of the conversation what  i was going to say though is um speaking  to even some of those who didn't support  the prime minister a couple of weeks ago  which i've done some of the some of the  the names which would be most  recognizable with people who've been  critical everyone now is in the mood of  let's give the prime minister the  opportunity the space the time to uh  make sure that he can carry on with some  of the uh work that he's doing we just  saw him in kiev a couple of days ago  and as president zielinski um says where  britain goes other follow others follow  with regard to  the war in ukraine i think he's showing  great international leadership there  he's going to help us get through these  challenges that we're facing at home  including um things like um cost of  living and some of the things we've been  doing on the railways prior to the  strikes like making sure that we we have  offers available for people to buy to  attract people back to the railways  through the through the sale and and  many many other things high employment  levels these are the things which really  matter to people so you know i just  think uh i i agree with some with some  of those who who didn't support the  prime minister we need to give uh the  opportunity uh for this government to do  what it was elected to do  notwithstanding that it had two years of  a huge uh health crisis to deal with as  well and looking back on that the prime  minister got us the injections first the  jabs first he got us out of lockdown  first in the world and those things are  things that this prime minister achieve  above anybody else  understood we are out of time at grant  chaps uh thank you very much for being  on the program this morning  thank you thanks so much
 

